---
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import RelatedLinks from "../components/RelatedLinks.astro";
import { breadcrumbLd, faqLd } from "../lib/schema";

const faqs = [
	{
		q: "Why 65% and 85%?",
		a: "They are planning buffers on this site, not FAA numbers. 65% of the published limit is caution; 85% or a gust at the limit is no-go. Spec sheets are not warranties.",
	},
	{
		q: "Why three heights?",
		a: "10 m is takeoff. 80 m is a typical cruise. 120 m is about the US recreational ceiling. A single surface wind reading misses the top of the flight.",
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="How the go / no-go call works — Drone Max Wind"
	description="Plain-English explanation of drone wind ratings, the 15% safety buffer, and free 10 m / 80 m / 120 m Open-Meteo wind."
	jsonLd={[
		breadcrumbLd([
			{ name: "Home", href: "/" },
			{ name: "How it works", href: "/how-it-works" },
		]),
		faqLd(faqs),
	]}
>
	<Header />
	<main class="page-wrap max-w-3xl py-16">
		<p class="font-mono text-caption uppercase tracking-wide text-mute">Method</p>
		<h1 class="mt-3 text-display-lg font-semibold text-ink">How we call go or no-go.</h1>
		<p class="mt-5 text-body-lg text-body">
			No log wind profiles. No paywalled 400 ft chart. Three numbers, your drone's published
			limit, and a buffer so gusts do not get the last word.
		</p>

		<ol class="mt-12 grid gap-8">
			<li>
				<h2 class="text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink">1. You pick the airframe.</h2>
				<p class="mt-2 text-body">
					Each model stores a max sustained wind in m/s from the manufacturer when they
					publish one. Pick a{" "}
					<a href="/dji-mini-4-pro-wind-resistance-calculator" class="text-link">Mini 4 Pro</a>,{" "}
					<a href="/dji-air-3s-wind-resistance-calculator" class="text-link">Air 3S</a>, or
					browse the{" "}
					<a href="/models" class="text-link">model database</a>. Holy Stone and a few cinema
					airframes are class estimates — the calculator labels those so you are not guessing
					in silence.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<h2 class="text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink">2. We pull three heights.</h2>
				<p class="mt-2 text-body">
					Open-Meteo hourly wind at 10 m, 80 m, and 120 m, plus 10 m gusts. 120 m is about 394
					ft — the US recreational ceiling. Gusts at 80 m and 120 m are scaled from the 10 m
					gust factor. That is conservative, not magic.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<h2 class="text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink">3. Peak wind vs the rating.</h2>
				<p class="mt-2 text-body">
					We take the worse of sustained and gust at each height. Under 65% of the rating:
					safe to fly. 65–85%: caution. 85% or more, or any gust at the limit: do not fly.
					That maps to DJI{" "}
					<a href="/drone-wind-resistance-levels" class="text-link">Level 4 vs Level 5</a>{" "}
					on the{" "}
					<a href="/drone-wind-resistance" class="text-link">wind resistance</a> spec. The
					extra 15% is for rotor wash, ridge rotors, and the fact that spec sheets are not
					warranties.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ol>

		<div class="mt-12 rounded-md bg-canvas p-6 shadow-card">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink">What this is not.</h2>
			<p class="mt-2 text-body">
				Not LAANC. Not a TFR check. Not a substitute for looking at the trees. If the app says
				go and the windsock says no, the windsock wins.
			</p>
		</div>
		<p class="mt-8">
			<a href="/#calculator" class="font-medium text-link">Run a field check →</a>
		</p>
		<Faq items={faqs} />
		<RelatedLinks
			links={[
				{ href: "/models", label: "Model database" },
				{ href: "/compare", label: "Compare airframes" },
				{ href: "/drone-wind-resistance", label: "What wind resistance means" },
				{ href: "/drone-weather", label: "Drone weather" },
			]}
		/>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Layout>
